{"causes":["The power struggle between Cyrus the Younger and Artaxerxes II led to the recruitment of Greek mercenaries for the Persian throne.","The decline of Persian authority and internal divisions prompted Cyrus's bid for power, necessitating the support of foreign soldiers.","The socio-political landscape of the region, marked by shifting alliances and conflicts, created an environment ripe for military expeditions."],"effects":["Anabasis provided a detailed account of the Greek mercenary experience, influencing subsequent military writings and historical narratives.","The text solidified Xenophon’s reputation as a historian and philosopher, impacting the study of ethics in leadership and warfare.","The journey of the Ten Thousand highlighted the challenges of mercenary warfare, shaping the future of military recruitment and strategy in ancient Greece."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"xenophon","sourceName":"Xenophon","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"anabasis","targetName":"Anabasis","context":"Xenophon authored Anabasis, detailing the journey of the Ten Thousand after the Battle of Cunaxa in 401 BC."},{"sourceSlug":"ten-thousand","sourceName":"The Ten Thousand","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"battle-of-cunaxa","targetName":"Battle of Cunaxa","context":"The Ten Thousand participated in the Battle of Cunaxa in 401 BC, which led to the death of Cyrus the Younger."},{"sourceSlug":"anabasis","sourceName":"Anabasis","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"alexander-the-great","targetName":"Alexander the Great","context":"Anabasis influenced Alexander the Great, who drew lessons from Xenophon's account of leadership and military strategy."},{"sourceSlug":"mesopotamia","sourceName":"Mesopotamia","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"anabasis","targetName":"Anabasis","context":"Anabasis recounts events that occurred in Mesopotamia during the retreat of the Ten Thousand between 401 and 400 BC."},{"sourceSlug":"greek-mercenaries","sourceName":"Greek Mercenaries","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"anabasis","targetName":"Anabasis","context":"The experiences of Greek mercenaries during their retreat are framed by Xenophon in Anabasis, emphasizing the human experience of war."}],"places":[{"name":"Cunaxa, Iraq","role":"Battle site"},{"name":"Babylon, Iraq","role":"Major city visited"},{"name":"Anatolia, Turkey","role":"Route taken"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Anabasis","type":"Reference article","slug":"anabasis"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q73112","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anabasis_(Xenophon)"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Anabasis contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Anabasis (Greek: Ἀνάβασις, 'The March Upcountry' or 'The Expedition') is the narrative written by the Athenian historian and soldier Xenophon (c. 430–354 BCE), describing the doomed expedition of the Ten Thousand — a Greek mercenary army hired by Cyrus the Younger to seize the Persian throne from his brother Artaxerxes II — and the remarkable 1,500-mile retreat of the survivors through hostile territory in Asia Minor after Cyrus's death at the Battle of Cunaxa (401 BCE). It is one of the greatest adventure narratives of antiquity, a foundational text of Greek prose literature, and a military classic that directly influenced Alexander the Great's Persian campaigns and, through the centuries, countless subsequent military expeditions.

After Cyrus was killed at Cunaxa and the Greek generals treacherously murdered by the Persians, the Ten Thousand found themselves stranded deep in Persia, surrounded by enemies, without leadership, supply lines, or guides. The soldiers elected new leaders (including Xenophon himself, despite being only a civilian volunteer), and under constant harassment by Persian forces, Kardouchian mountain peoples, and harsh terrain and weather, they fought their way northward to the Black Sea coast — the famous cry 'Thalatta! Thalatta!' ('The sea! The sea!') when the first men reached the crest and saw the Black Sea below them is one of the most celebrated moments in ancient literature.

The Anabasis's military significance is exceptional: Alexander the Great reportedly kept a copy annotated by Aristotle and used the expedition as proof that Greek armies could penetrate the Persian heartland. Napoleon cited it as a model of military leadership under adversity. It also provided the earliest sustained Greek ethnographic account of the peoples of Anatolia, Armenia, and the Black Sea coast, influencing Herodotean geography. Xenophon's account of democratic military decision-making — the soldiers voting on strategy, selecting leaders by merit — is also a remarkable document of Greek democratic practices in extremis.","subjects":["Ancient Greece","Classical Era","Military History","Persian Empire","Greek Literature","Ancient History","Xenophon","10 Thousand"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Anabasis shaped the understanding of military expeditions and leadership ethics, influencing both historical narratives and military strategies in subsequent centuries.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
